INT. TELEGRAPH RELAY STATION, RICE-PADDY DIKE - NIGHT

A mud-brick hut on stilts. Lantern. Wires leading out the window into the dark. Frogs outside.

LIEUTENANT KOJI NAKAGAWA, 30s, sits at the key with a headset and a pencil behind his ear. Corporal Obuya MWANGI, 20s, squats by the door with a carbine. An old LOCAL WOMAN, MAI LIEN, 60s, sets down a pot of tea at the table and steps back into the shadows.

He taps a message. The sounder clatters back. He writes it down.

Mwangi watches the woman. She watches the floor. The lieutenant tears the page off the pad and folds it into his breast pocket.

KOJI
Corporal. Tea.

MWANGI
Sir.

He pours two cups. He sets one in front of the lieutenant and keeps the other, cold, in his hand. He does not drink. The woman looks at the cup, then at the door, then away.

KOJI
(without turning)
Ask her how far the next village is.

MWANGI
She doesn't speak English, sir.

KOJI
Ask anyway.

Mwangi turns, mouths a few halting words in her language. She answers with two fingers, then three, then folds her hands. He looks back at the lieutenant.

MWANGI
Two hours. Maybe three.

KOJI
Thank you.

He taps again. The sounder answers. He writes. Outside, the frogs stop all at once. Mwangi's thumb finds the safety on the carbine. The woman's fingers find the rim of the teapot and rest there, perfectly still.

KOJI (CONT'D)
(very quietly)
Corporal.

MWANGI
I hear it, sir.

KOJI
Finish your tea.

Mwangi lifts the cup and drinks it to the bottom. Koji lays down the pencil. The lantern flame leans, leans, and steadies.
